// Generated from the reviewed question bank. Edit the source JSON, not this file.
export default [{"id":"sci-1-001","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks are what kind of animal?","why":"Sharks breathe underwater with gills, like other fish. Whales and dolphins breathe air, so they are mammals.","hint":"Think about how a shark breathes underwater.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["A bird","A mammal","A fish"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-1-002","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the butterfly's life in order, from the very start.","why":"A butterfly changes shape completely. It hatches as a caterpillar and grows before it turns into a butterfly.","hint":"A butterfly begins very small.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Egg","Caterpillar","Chrysalis","Butterfly"]},{"id":"sci-1-003","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What is the Sun?","why":"The Sun is a star, a giant ball of hot glowing gas. Planets and moons only shine by reflecting light.","hint":"It makes its own light.","tags":["space"],"opts":["A star","A moon","A planet"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-1-004","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Dinosaurs like T. rex lived at the same time as people.","why":"Big dinosaurs like T. rex died out about 66 million years ago, long before the first people.","hint":"Think about how old fossils are.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-1-005","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"number","q":"How many hearts does an octopus have?","why":"An octopus has three hearts: two pump blood to the gills and one pumps blood to the body.","hint":"It is more than you have, but fewer than five.","tags":["ocean","animals"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-1-006","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What job do roots do for a plant?","why":"Roots drink water from the soil and hold the plant in place. Flowers make seeds and attract bees.","hint":"Roots grow underground.","tags":[],"opts":["Make the seeds for new plants","Drink water from the soil","Attract bees to the flower"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-1-007","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is a PULL?","why":"A pull moves something toward you. Kicking and hitting push things away.","hint":"A pull brings something closer to you.","tags":["machines","baseball"],"opts":["Opening a drawer","Hitting a baseball","Kicking a ball"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-1-008","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these animals in order from smallest to biggest.","why":"Each animal is bigger than the one before. A blue whale is longer than two school buses.","hint":"Think about how much space each one takes up.","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["Ant","Cat","Horse","Blue whale"]},{"id":"sci-1-009","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The Moon makes its own light.","why":"The Moon shines because it bounces back light from the Sun. Only stars make their own light.","hint":"What lights up the Moon at night?","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-1-010","topic":"science","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the life of a plant in order, from the start.","why":"A seed sprouts roots and a stem, grows into a small plant, and then makes flowers.","hint":"Everything begins as a seed.","tags":[],"opts":["Seed","Sprout","Small plant","Flower"]},{"id":"sci-2-001","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which part of a shark's body sweeps side to side to push it forward?","why":"The tail fin swishes back and forth and drives the shark through the water. Gills are for breathing.","hint":"Think about what pushes a swimming fish forward.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["Sharp teeth","The gills","Tail fin"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-2-002","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put the water cycle in order, starting with the Sun heating the sea.","why":"Heat turns sea water into invisible vapor. High up it cools into cloud drops, and rain returns the water.","hint":"What happens first: warming or cooling?","tags":["weather","ocean"],"opts":["Sun heats the water","Water vapor rises","Vapor cools into clouds","Rain falls"]},{"id":"sci-2-003","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Triceratops was a herbivore. What did it eat?","why":"Herbivores eat only plants. Tyrannosaurus rex was a carnivore and ate meat.","hint":"Herb- sounds like herbs and plants.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Other dinosaurs","Plants","Fish"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-2-004","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Sound is made when something vibrates, or wiggles back and forth very fast.","why":"Pluck a rubber band and you can see it wiggle. Those fast wiggles make the sound you hear.","hint":"Touch your throat while you hum.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-2-005","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"number","q":"How many legs does an insect have?","why":"All insects have six legs. Spiders have eight legs, so they are not insects.","hint":"Count the legs on an ant.","tags":["animals"],"a":6},{"id":"sci-2-006","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Why is it dark at night?","why":"Earth spins like a top. When your part turns away from the Sun, it is night.","hint":"Earth keeps turning around.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Our side of Earth turns from the Sun","The Sun gets switched off until morning","The Moon moves in front of the Sun"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-2-007","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put these planets in order, starting closest to the Sun.","why":"A memory trick: My Very Eager Mother. Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars come first.","hint":"Mercury is the closest.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Mercury","Venus","Earth","Mars"]},{"id":"sci-2-008","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Friction slows down a baseball that rolls across the grass.","why":"Friction is rubbing between two things. The grass rubs the ball and slows it until it stops.","hint":"Why does a rolling ball stop?","tags":["baseball","machines"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-2-009","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which body part pumps blood around your body?","why":"The heart is a strong muscle that pumps blood. Lungs bring in air and the stomach breaks down food.","hint":"Put your hand on your chest.","tags":["body"],"opts":["Stomach","Lungs","Heart"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-2-010","topic":"science","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put a frog's life in order, from the start.","why":"Tadpoles breathe with gills, then grow legs and lungs and change into frogs.","hint":"Tadpoles live in water.","tags":["animals"],"opts":["Egg","Tadpole","Tadpole with legs","Adult frog"]},{"id":"sci-3-001","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A shark's skin feels like sandpaper. What covers it?","why":"Shark skin is covered with tiny scales shaped like teeth. They help water flow smoothly past the shark.","hint":"Think rough, not soft.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Thick layers of fur","Hard shell plates","Tooth-like scales","Soft, fluffy feathers"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-3-002","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"What is a fossil?","why":"Fossils are bones, shells, footprints or other traces of living things preserved in rock for a very long time.","hint":"Fossils show us living things from long ago.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["A trace of ancient life in rock","A rock made from cooled lava","A seashell that washed up this year","A plant that grows deep in caves"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-3-003","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these giant planets in order, starting closest to the Sun.","why":"The four giant planets lie beyond Mars and the asteroid belt. Jupiter is nearest the Sun and Neptune is farthest.","hint":"They all come after Mars.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Jupiter","Saturn","Uranus","Neptune"]},{"id":"sci-3-004","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A seesaw is an example of which simple machine?","why":"A lever is a bar that turns around a point called a fulcrum. A seesaw turns on its middle.","hint":"It tips back and forth on a middle point.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["Wedge","Lever","Pulley","Screw"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-3-005","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"In a fair test, you change only one thing at a time.","why":"If you change two things, you cannot tell which one caused the result.","hint":"Think about testing two paper airplanes.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-3-006","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"number","q":"Water boils at about how many degrees Celsius at sea level?","why":"Water boils at 100 degrees Celsius and freezes at 0. That is 212 and 32 degrees Fahrenheit.","hint":"It is a nice round number.","tags":["weather"],"a":100},{"id":"sci-3-007","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Coral reefs are built by tiny animals. What are they called?","why":"Coral polyps build hard skeletons around themselves. Over a very long time, many polyps build a reef.","hint":"They are tiny and stay in one place.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Jellyfish","Sea stars","Sponges","Coral polyps"],"a":3},{"id":"sci-3-008","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks have hard bones inside their bodies.","why":"A shark's skeleton is made of cartilage, the bendy stuff in your ears and nose, not bone.","hint":"Feel the end of your nose.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-3-009","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Why does the Moon seem to change shape each night?","why":"The Moon is always half lit by the Sun. As it orbits Earth, we see more or less of that lit half.","hint":"The Moon itself never changes shape.","tags":["space"],"opts":["We see changing amounts of its lit half","Earth's shadow covers different parts of it","Clouds in space cover different parts of it","It melts and regrows every single month"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-3-010","topic":"science","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put this ocean food chain in order, starting with the energy source.","why":"Seaweed uses sunlight to make food, small fish eat the seaweed, and sharks eat the fish.","hint":"Where does all the energy start?","tags":["ocean","sharks"],"opts":["Sunlight","Seaweed","Small fish","Shark"]},{"id":"sci-4-001","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A shark loses a tooth while biting. What happens next?","why":"Sharks have rows of spare teeth behind the front ones. They move forward like a conveyor belt.","hint":"Sharks have many rows of teeth.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["A new tooth grows back in one day","A spare tooth moves up to replace it","Nothing happens; sharks keep all teeth","It stops eating until the tooth regrows"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-4-002","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Long lines of fossil dinosaur footprints can show scientists what?","why":"Footprints are trace fossils. Their spacing shows how fast the dinosaur walked, and their size hints at its body.","hint":"Footprints record movement, not color.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["What it ate for its last lunch","What sounds it made when it roared","How it moved and how big it was","What color its skin and eyes were"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-4-003","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put Earth's layers in order, starting at the outside.","why":"We live on the thin crust. Below it are the thick mantle, the liquid outer core and the solid inner core.","hint":"Go from where we live down to the center.","tags":[],"opts":["Crust","Mantle","Outer core","Inner core"]},{"id":"sci-4-004","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A hit baseball flies, slows down, then falls. Which forces act on it in the air?","why":"Gravity pulls the ball down and air pushes against it. The bat's push ends the moment they separate.","hint":"Think about what pushes back as the ball moves.","tags":["baseball","machines"],"opts":["Magnetism and gravity","Only gravity","Gravity and a lasting push from the bat","Gravity and air resistance"],"a":3},{"id":"sci-4-005","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"On the Moon, a hammer and a feather dropped together land at the same time.","why":"The Moon has no air to slow the feather. Apollo 15 astronaut David Scott showed this in 1971.","hint":"What would slow the feather on Earth?","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-4-006","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"number","q":"How many chambers (rooms) does a human heart have?","why":"Two small chambers on top take in blood. Two strong chambers on the bottom pump it out.","hint":"Two on top, two on the bottom.","tags":["body"],"a":4},{"id":"sci-4-007","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"What mainly causes ocean tides?","why":"The Moon's gravity tugs on the ocean and makes the water bulge. The Sun adds a smaller pull.","hint":"Something far away in space is pulling.","tags":["ocean","space"],"opts":["The pull of the Moon's gravity","Strong winds over the sea","Rivers pushing water out to sea","Whales swimming in groups"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-4-008","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put the ocean zones in order, from the surface down.","why":"Light fades with depth. Below about 1,000 meters it is completely dark, and the water is near freezing.","hint":"The sunlit zone is the one near the top.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Sunlit zone","Twilight zone","Midnight zone","Abyss"]},{"id":"sci-4-009","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Sound travels faster through water than through air.","why":"Water's tiny particles are packed closer together, so sound passes along them faster. That helps whales call far away.","hint":"Think about how whales communicate.","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-4-010","topic":"science","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"You see wet footprints by a pool and say, \"Someone just swam here.\" What is that?","why":"An observation is what you sense, like wet footprints. An inference is a smart explanation based on it.","hint":"Is it what you saw, or what you figured out?","tags":[],"opts":["A measurement","An observation","An inference","A prediction"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-5-001","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A nurse shark can rest on the sea floor without swimming. How does it still breathe?","why":"Some sharks can use their mouth and throat to pump water over the gills. Others must swim to keep water flowing.","hint":"Gills need a steady flow of water.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["It breathes through its skin","It pumps water over its gills","It uses a blowhole like a whale","It gulps air at the surface"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-5-002","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which living animals are the closest relatives of Tyrannosaurus rex?","why":"Fossils show many meat-eating dinosaurs had feathers and wishbones. Birds are their living descendants.","hint":"Think feathers and wishbones.","tags":["dinosaurs","animals"],"opts":["Elephants","Sharks","Lizards","Birds"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-5-003","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these planets in order from smallest to largest.","why":"Mercury and Mars are small and rocky. Earth is bigger, and Neptune and Jupiter are giant planets.","hint":"Rocky planets are smaller than giant planets.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Mercury","Mars","Earth","Neptune","Jupiter"]},{"id":"sci-5-004","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A bat hits a baseball and the ball speeds up. Where did its extra motion energy come from?","why":"Energy passes from the moving bat to the ball. That is why a faster swing sends the ball farther.","hint":"What was moving before they touched?","tags":["baseball","machines"],"opts":["The ball's own weight","The swinging bat","The air around it","The noise of the crowd"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-5-005","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Earth has seasons because it moves closer to and farther from the Sun.","why":"Seasons come from Earth's tilt. When your part of Earth leans toward the Sun, it gets stronger sunlight and summer.","hint":"Think about Earth's tilt.","tags":["space","weather"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-5-006","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A pitch travels 18 meters in half a second. How many meters does it travel each second at that speed?","why":"Half a second goes into one second twice, so double the distance: 18 times 2 is 36 meters.","hint":"Double the distance.","tags":["baseball"],"a":36},{"id":"sci-5-007","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Why does ocean water taste salty?","why":"Water evaporates from the sea but the salts stay behind, so over millions of years the ocean got salty.","hint":"Salt comes from rocks.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Fish give off salt as they swim","Waves mix in salt from the sea air","Rivers wash rock salts into the sea","The Sun bakes salt into the sea water"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-5-008","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these in order from smallest to largest.","why":"Cells join to make tissues, tissues make organs, and organs work together in systems of the body.","hint":"Start with the tiniest building block.","tags":["body"],"opts":["Cell","Tissue","Organ","Body system","Whole body"]},{"id":"sci-5-009","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Sound can travel through outer space just like it travels through air.","why":"Space is almost empty, so there is nothing to carry the vibrations. In space, no one can hear a rocket roar.","hint":"Sound needs something to wiggle.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-5-010","topic":"science","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"You test whether warm water melts ice faster. Which should stay the SAME in every cup?","why":"The thing you change is the water temperature. The ice size must be the same so the test is fair.","hint":"Keep everything the same except one thing.","tags":[],"opts":["The size of the ice cubes","Which cup you pick","The water temperature","The time it takes to melt"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-6-001","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks have jelly-filled pores on their snouts called ampullae of Lorenzini. What do they sense?","why":"Living animals give off a faint electric field. Sharks can feel it, even when the prey is hidden in sand.","hint":"It is a sense humans do not have.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Faint light from deep in the water","How salty the water around it is","Sound waves coming from far away","Faint electric signals from prey"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-6-002","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Similar dinosaur fossils are found on continents now far apart. What helps explain this?","why":"Continents slowly drift on moving plates. Long ago many lands were connected, so animals could walk between them.","hint":"Think about plate tectonics.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Dinosaurs swam across oceans each year","Wind blew bones across the sea","Those lands were once joined together","Fossils form in the same way everywhere"],"a":2},{"id":"sci-6-003","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put the Moon's phases in order, starting at new moon.","why":"The lit part grows to a full moon in about two weeks, then shrinks through last quarter back to new.","hint":"The lit part grows first, then shrinks.","tags":["space"],"opts":["New moon","Waxing crescent","First quarter","Full moon","Last quarter"]},{"id":"sci-6-004","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A curveball curves in flight. What makes it curve?","why":"Spin drags air around the ball, so the pressure is unequal on its sides. The higher pressure pushes it sideways.","hint":"Think about the ball's spin.","tags":["baseball","machines"],"opts":["Gravity pulls harder on spinning balls","Spin changes air pressure around it","One side of the ball gets much heavier","Strong wind always pushes it sideways"],"a":1},{"id":"sci-6-005","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Mercury's year (one trip around the Sun) is longer than Earth's year.","why":"Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun, so it travels a shorter path much faster. Its year is only about 88 days.","hint":"Closer planets orbit faster.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"sci-6-006","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Sound travels about 340 meters each second in air. You hear thunder 3 seconds after you see lightning. About how many meters away was the lightning?","why":"You see lightning almost instantly, so the delay is the sound's trip. 340 times 3 equals 1,020 meters.","hint":"Multiply the speed by the time.","tags":["weather"],"a":1020},{"id":"sci-6-007","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The deeper you dive in the ocean, the more the water presses on you. Why?","why":"The water above you has weight, and the deeper you go, the more of it you carry. Divers feel pressure in their ears.","hint":"Think about a pile of books on you.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Sea creatures push you down","The water gets saltier and sticky","Deep water is always colder","More water is stacked above you"],"a":3},{"id":"sci-6-008","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put the life of a star like our Sun in order.","why":"A star forms from a cloud, shines steadily for billions of years, swells into a red giant, then shrinks to a white dwarf.","hint":"Stars are born in clouds.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Cloud of gas and dust","Steady star like our Sun","Red giant","White dwarf"]},{"id":"sci-6-009","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Salt water freezes at a lower temperature than fresh water.","why":"Salt gets in the way of ice crystals forming. The ocean must be colder than 0 degrees Celsius to freeze.","hint":"Why do we put salt on icy roads?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"sci-6-010","topic":"science","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A student grows one bean plant in sun and one in the dark and decides light helps beans grow. What would make the test more trustworthy?","why":"One plant could grow badly by chance. Using many plants in each place makes the pattern easier to trust.","hint":"One try could be luck.","tags":[],"opts":["Test several plants in each place","Water only the sunny plant","Use a bigger pot for the sunny plant","Keep only the best-looking plant"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-1-001","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A whale swims north. Which way is the opposite direction?","why":"North and south are opposites, and so are east and west.","hint":"Picture a compass: what is across from north?","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["West","South","East"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-1-002","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is a NEED, not just a want?","why":"Needs are things people must have to live, like food, water and shelter. Wants are nice extras.","hint":"What do you need to stay alive?","tags":["money"],"opts":["A video game","A toy","Food"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-1-003","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these places in order from smallest to biggest.","why":"Maps zoom out like this: a room sits inside a house, a house in a city, and a city in a country.","hint":"Each place fits inside the next one.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Your room","Your house","Your city","Your country"]},{"id":"wor-1-004","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Penguins live at the North Pole.","why":"Penguins live in the Southern Hemisphere, like Antarctica. Polar bears are the ones that live near the North Pole.","hint":"Which pole is in the far south?","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-1-005","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"number","q":"Most maps used in the United States show how many continents?","why":"The seven continents are Africa, Antarctica, Asia, Australia, Europe, North America and South America.","hint":"Count them on a globe.","tags":["maps"],"a":7},{"id":"wor-1-006","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The Great Pyramids were built long ago in the desert of which continent?","why":"The Great Pyramids stand in Egypt, in northern Africa. Ancient Egyptians built them as tombs for their kings.","hint":"Egypt is next to the Sahara.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Africa","Antarctica","South America"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-1-007","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Giant tortoises live on the Galápagos Islands. Which ocean are the islands in?","why":"The Galápagos Islands sit in the Pacific Ocean, off the west coast of South America.","hint":"They are west of South America.","tags":["ocean","animals","maps"],"opts":["Arctic Ocean","Pacific Ocean","Atlantic Ocean"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-1-008","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the compass directions in order going clockwise, starting at north.","why":"Clockwise is the way clock hands move. A trick: Never Eat Soggy Waffles.","hint":"Think of a clock face.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["North","East","South","West"]},{"id":"wor-1-009","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"In Spanish, the word \"tiburón\" means which animal?","why":"Tiburón is Spanish for shark. Ballena means whale and tortuga means turtle.","hint":"Think of a fin sticking out of the water.","tags":["sharks","animals"],"opts":["Turtle","Whale","Shark"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-1-010","topic":"world","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these steps in order for buying a baseball glove.","why":"You need to earn money first, then save it until you have enough, and then spend it.","hint":"You cannot spend money you do not have.","tags":["baseball","money"],"opts":["Earn some money","Save it up","Buy the glove"]},{"id":"wor-2-001","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Emperor penguins raise their chicks on which icy continent?","why":"Antarctica is a cold continent at the bottom of the world, where emperor penguins gather to raise chicks.","hint":"It is at the South Pole.","tags":["ocean","animals","maps"],"opts":["North America","Antarctica","Australia"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-2-002","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"What does a compass rose on a map show?","why":"The compass rose points out north, south, east and west so you can read the map.","hint":"It looks like a star with letters.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Directions","Mountain heights","Store prices"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-2-003","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put these four oceans in order from largest to smallest.","why":"The Pacific covers about a third of Earth. The Arctic Ocean is the smallest of these four.","hint":"The Pacific is biggest, the Arctic coldest and smallest.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["Pacific Ocean","Atlantic Ocean","Indian Ocean","Arctic Ocean"]},{"id":"wor-2-004","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The country Ecuador is named after a line around the middle of Earth. What is it?","why":"Ecuador is the Spanish word for equator. The country sits right on that line.","hint":"Spanish: ecuador. English: ...","tags":["maps"],"opts":["The Date Line","The Equator","The Prime Meridian"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-2-005","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The Hawaiian Islands are in the Pacific Ocean.","why":"Hawaii is a chain of islands in the middle of the Pacific Ocean, far from any continent.","hint":"Think of surfing and volcanoes.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-2-006","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"number","q":"On a map legend, 1 tree symbol means 10 real trees. A forest shows 3 tree symbols. How many real trees are there?","why":"A map legend explains what symbols mean. Three symbols times 10 trees each is 30 trees.","hint":"Count by tens.","tags":["maps"],"a":30},{"id":"wor-2-007","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which famous tower is in Paris, France?","why":"The Eiffel Tower stands in Paris. The Leaning Tower is in Pisa, Italy, and Big Ben is in London.","hint":"It is made of iron lattice.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Big Ben","The Leaning Tower","The Eiffel Tower"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-2-008","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The Bahamas are famous for being made of many what?","why":"The Bahamas are a chain of hundreds of islands in the Atlantic Ocean, with warm, clear water around them.","hint":"Picture palm trees and beaches with turquoise water.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["Islands","Deserts","Volcanoes"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-2-009","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"All deserts are hot and sandy.","why":"A desert is a place with very little rain. Antarctica is a cold desert covered in ice.","hint":"Deserts are about rain, not heat.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"wor-2-010","topic":"world","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put the steps of running a lemonade stand in order.","why":"You pay for supplies first (your cost), then make and sell the product, and finally count what you earned.","hint":"You make it before you sell it.","tags":["money","food"],"opts":["Buy lemons","Make lemonade","Sell cups","Count your money"]},{"id":"wor-3-001","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The Galápagos Islands are part of which country?","why":"The Galápagos belong to Ecuador, even though they are about 1,000 kilometers out in the Pacific Ocean.","hint":"They sit in the Pacific, west of South America.","tags":["ocean","animals","maps"],"opts":["Peru","Chile","Ecuador","Colombia"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-3-002","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"What does a map scale help you find out?","why":"A scale like 1 cm = 10 km turns map distances into real distances. A compass rose shows direction.","hint":"It looks like a little ruler.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Which way is north on the map","How tall the mountains are","The names of all the countries","Real distances between places"],"a":3},{"id":"wor-3-003","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these buildings in order from oldest to newest.","why":"The Great Pyramid is about 4,500 years old. The Eiffel Tower was finished in 1889, so it is the newest.","hint":"Ancient builders came before iron towers.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Great Pyramid of Giza","Parthenon in Athens","Colosseum in Rome","Eiffel Tower in Paris"]},{"id":"wor-3-004","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Profit is the money you have left after you...","why":"A lemonade stand earns $12 and spent $5 on supplies, so the profit is $7. Profit is earnings minus costs.","hint":"Earnings minus what you spent.","tags":["money"],"opts":["Spend it all","Pay your costs","Raise your price","Borrow more"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-3-005","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The Equator is a line that runs from the North Pole to the South Pole.","why":"The Equator runs around the middle of Earth, east to west. Lines from pole to pole are called meridians.","hint":"Does it circle the middle or go up and down?","tags":["maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-3-006","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"number","q":"You buy a used baseball glove for $12 and sell it for $20. How many dollars of profit do you make?","why":"Profit is what you sell for minus what you paid: 20 minus 12 equals 8.","hint":"Subtract the cost from the price.","tags":["baseball","money"],"a":8},{"id":"wor-3-007","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The Great Barrier Reef, a huge coral reef, is off the coast of which continent?","why":"The Great Barrier Reef lies off the northeast coast of Australia in the Coral Sea.","hint":"It is also called the Land Down Under.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["Australia","Africa","South America","Europe"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-3-008","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these cities in order from north to south.","why":"This is the Pacific coast of North America. Each city is a little farther south than the one before.","hint":"Canada is the farthest north.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Vancouver, Canada","Seattle","Portland","San Francisco"]},{"id":"wor-3-009","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The Hawaiian Islands were built by volcanoes rising from the sea floor.","why":"Lava built up from the ocean floor over millions of years until it poked above the surface as islands.","hint":"Think of Kilauea.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wor-3-010","topic":"world","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these cities in order from west to east.","why":"Honolulu is out in the Pacific Ocean. Heading east you reach the Pacific coast, the Rocky Mountains and the Atlantic coast.","hint":"Start in the Pacific Ocean.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Honolulu","Los Angeles","Denver","New York City"]},{"id":"wor-4-001","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The Equator divides Earth into which two halves?","why":"The Equator splits Earth into the Northern and Southern Hemispheres. The Prime Meridian splits east from west.","hint":"The Equator is the halfway line between the poles.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["A land half and a water half","The day side and the night side","Eastern and Western halves","Northern and Southern halves"],"a":3},{"id":"wor-4-002","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Mount Rainier, a snowy volcano near Seattle, belongs to which mountain range?","why":"The Cascades run from Canada through Washington and Oregon. The Rockies are farther east, and the Appalachians are on the other side of the country.","hint":"This range is in the Pacific Northwest.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Appalachian Mountains","Cascade Range","Sierra Nevada","Rocky Mountains"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-4-003","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put these climate zones in order, from the Equator toward the North Pole.","why":"It gets cooler as you travel away from the Equator. Tropical is warmest, temperate is in between, and polar is coldest.","hint":"Start where it is warmest.","tags":["maps","weather"],"opts":["Tropical zone","Temperate zone","Polar zone"]},{"id":"wor-4-004","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Only 5 rare baseball cards are for sale, but 50 kids want one. What will probably happen to the price?","why":"When a lot of people want something that is in short supply, sellers can ask for more money.","hint":"Think about how many cards there are compared to kids.","tags":["baseball","money"],"opts":["It goes up","It goes down","It stays the same","The cards become free"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-4-005","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The Equator passes through the Galápagos Islands.","why":"The line crosses Isabela Island, so the Galápagos lie in both the Northern and Southern Hemispheres.","hint":"Think about which country the islands belong to.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wor-4-006","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Florida is a peninsula. What does that mean?","why":"A peninsula is land that sticks out into the water. An island is surrounded by water on every side.","hint":"Look at Florida on a map.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["It has no coast or beaches","It stands on top of a mountain","Water touches it on three sides","Water surrounds it on every side"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-4-007","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"number","q":"A map scale says 1 cm = 5 km. A tagged shark's path is 4 cm long on the map. How many kilometers did it swim?","why":"Every centimeter on the map stands for 5 real kilometers. Four centimeters is 4 times 5.","hint":"Multiply the map distance by the scale.","tags":["sharks","ocean","maps"],"a":20},{"id":"wor-4-008","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Long ago, sailors steered at night by one star in the northern sky that hardly seems to move. What is it called?","why":"The North Star, Polaris, sits almost straight above the North Pole. Sailors knew it showed them north.","hint":"Its name tells you which way it points.","tags":["space","maps"],"opts":["The Evening Star","The Morning Star","The Dog Star","The North Star"],"a":3},{"id":"wor-4-009","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put these steps in order for how bread reaches a store.","why":"Goods pass through many workers before you buy them. Each step needs the one before it.","hint":"The wheat has to exist first.","tags":["food","money"],"opts":["Farmers grow wheat","Mill grinds wheat into flour","Bakery bakes the bread","Store sells the bread"]},{"id":"wor-4-010","topic":"world","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A bank pays you a little extra money for keeping your savings there. What is that extra money called?","why":"Interest is a small reward for letting the bank use your savings. The longer you save, the more it can grow.","hint":"It is money that your money earns.","tags":["money"],"opts":["Profit from selling","Interest","A tax","A price tag"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-5-001","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which imaginary lines run east to west and tell you how far north or south a place is?","why":"Latitude lines are like the rungs of a ladder. Longitude lines run from pole to pole.","hint":"Picture the rungs of a ladder.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Lines of longitude","Lines of scale","Lines of latitude","Lines of elevation"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-5-002","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A baseball game in New York starts at 7:00 PM New York time. New York is 3 hours ahead of Los Angeles. What hour is it in Los Angeles then?","why":"Places farther west have earlier clock times, because the Sun reaches the east first. Subtract 3 hours from 7.","hint":"Which way do you go on the clock?","tags":["baseball","maps"],"a":4},{"id":"wor-5-003","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"You have $10 and choose a baseball cap instead of a book. What is the book called in this choice?","why":"Opportunity cost is the next best thing you give up when you choose. It is not the money, but the lost choice.","hint":"It is what you gave up.","tags":["baseball","money"],"opts":["The opportunity cost","The interest you earn","The profit you earn","The budget you set"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-5-004","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these cities in order, from nearest to farthest from the Equator.","why":"Quito is almost on the Equator. Each city after it is farther north.","hint":"One of them sits almost on the Equator.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["Quito, Ecuador","Miami","Seattle","Anchorage, Alaska"]},{"id":"wor-5-005","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"If a storm destroys many orange crops, orange juice will probably cost more.","why":"Fewer oranges means a smaller supply. When something becomes scarce, sellers can charge a higher price.","hint":"Think about supply and price.","tags":["food","money"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-5-006","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Why are river deltas, where a river reaches the sea, often good for farming?","why":"Rivers carry bits of soil downstream. At the delta the water slows and drops it, making soil full of nutrients.","hint":"Think about what a slow river drops.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["The land is high and rocky","Rivers drop rich soil there","It never rains in deltas","Volcanoes heat the ground there"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-5-007","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The Pacific, Atlantic and Indian Oceans all touch a cold ocean that circles Antarctica. What is it called?","why":"The Southern Ocean wraps around Antarctica. The Arctic Ocean is near the North Pole.","hint":"Its name tells you its direction.","tags":["ocean","maps"],"opts":["Mediterranean Sea","Arctic Ocean","Caribbean Sea","Southern Ocean"],"a":3},{"id":"wor-5-008","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The Prime Meridian is the line of 0 degrees longitude.","why":"Longitude is measured east and west from the Prime Meridian. It passes through Greenwich, near London.","hint":"It is the starting line for longitude.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-5-009","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Why are the polar regions cold all year?","why":"Near the poles the Sun's rays hit at a low angle and cover a larger area, so each spot gets less heat.","hint":"Think about a flashlight shining at a slant.","tags":["weather","maps"],"opts":["They are the closest places to the Sun","Cold winds from space cool them","Sunlight arrives at a low slant","The Sun never shines on them at all"],"a":2},{"id":"wor-5-010","topic":"world","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Ancient Egyptian builders used ramps and levers to help move heavy stones. A ramp is which simple machine?","why":"An inclined plane is a slanted surface. It lets you raise a load with less push over a longer distance.","hint":"It is a slanted surface.","tags":["machines"],"opts":["Inclined plane","Pulley","Wheel and axle","Screw"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-6-001","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Quito, Ecuador, sits almost on the Equator but has cool, spring-like weather. Why?","why":"Air gets colder as elevation rises. Quito is about 2,850 meters up, so the Equator's heat is cooled by the altitude.","hint":"Think about how it feels at the top of a tall mountain.","tags":["maps","weather"],"opts":["Cold ocean water surrounds it","It sits farther from the Sun","It sits high in the Andes","Snow falls on it all year long"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"wor-6-002","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Each time zone is about 15 degrees of longitude wide. Earth is 360 degrees around. About how many time zones fit around the whole Earth?","why":"360 divided by 15 is 24. That matches the 24 hours in a day, because Earth turns once a day.","hint":"Divide 360 by 15.","tags":["maps","space"],"a":24},{"id":"wor-6-003","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Seattle gets lots of rain, but the land just east of the Cascade Mountains is dry. Why?","why":"Moist Pacific air rises up the mountains, cools and drops rain. The air that descends the other side is dry.","hint":"What happens to air as it rises over a mountain?","tags":["weather","maps"],"opts":["Mountains squeeze rain from the air","The ocean is much colder east of them","Rain only ever falls near the coast","Dry winds blow in from the Atlantic"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-6-004","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these ways of finding direction at sea in order, from oldest to newest.","why":"Sailors used stars first. Then they added the compass, and the sextant measured star angles. GPS satellites came last.","hint":"Satellites are the newest invention here.","tags":["maps","ocean"],"opts":["Watching the stars","Magnetic compass","Sextant","Satellite GPS"]},{"id":"wor-6-005","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A place at 30 degrees South latitude and 120 degrees West longitude is in both the Southern and Western Hemispheres.","why":"South latitude means south of the Equator. West longitude means west of the Prime Meridian.","hint":"Read the directions in each number.","tags":["maps"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-6-006","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Why did people invent money instead of only trading goods (barter)?","why":"If you have fish and want wheat, the wheat seller must also want fish. Money works for everyone.","hint":"Think about finding someone who wants your fish.","tags":["money","ocean"],"opts":["Goods were always too heavy to carry","Rulers made direct trading illegal","Only rich people were allowed to trade","Both must want what the other has"],"a":3},{"id":"wor-6-007","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Why do islands far from any continent, like the Galápagos, often have animals found nowhere else?","why":"Few animals reach distant islands. Those that do live apart from their relatives and slowly change.","hint":"Think about being cut off from the mainland.","tags":["ocean","animals","maps"],"opts":["Volcanoes there create new animals","Isolated animals slowly change","Ocean water changes their bodies","Islands have no predators or diseases"],"a":1},{"id":"wor-6-008","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these US time zones in order, from where the Sun rises first to where it rises last.","why":"Earth turns from west to east, so the Sun reaches the east first. Noon comes to Eastern first and Pacific last.","hint":"The Sun rises in the east.","tags":["maps","space"],"opts":["Eastern","Central","Mountain","Pacific"]},{"id":"wor-6-009","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Antarctic scientists study air bubbles trapped in very old ice. What can the bubbles tell them?","why":"Falling snow squeezes air into ice layers. Deeper layers hold older air, like pages in a very old book.","hint":"Older ice holds older air.","tags":["weather","maps"],"opts":["What the air was like long ago","Which animals once lived on the ice","How deep the ocean is below it","Which ships have sailed nearby"],"a":0},{"id":"wor-6-010","topic":"world","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A bakery raises the price of its cookies and fewer people buy them. Which idea does this show?","why":"When something costs more, fewer people want to buy it at that price. That is how demand usually works.","hint":"Think about how shoppers react to a higher price.","tags":["money","food"],"opts":["Profit is always the same as price","Supply always exactly matches demand","Demand often falls when prices rise","Bakeries never pay for ingredients"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-1-001","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The blue whale is huge. Which word means almost the same as huge?","why":"Huge means very big. Small is the opposite, and fast is about speed, not size.","hint":"Picture the biggest animal in the sea.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["very big","very small","very fast"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-1-002","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Sam put on his coat, hat, and gloves. Then he ran outside to play in the snow. What was the weather like?","why":"Coats, gloves, and snow are clues for cold weather. A hot day would not need them.","hint":"Think about why someone wears gloves.","tags":["weather"],"opts":["hot","cold","warm and dry"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-1-003","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put the story in order.","why":"She must pick up the bat first. Then she swings, the ball flies, and the crowd cheers.","hint":"What must happen before she can swing?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Mia picked up her bat.","She swung at the ball.","The ball flew over the fence.","Everyone cheered."]},{"id":"rea-1-004","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"'Quiet' and 'loud' mean almost the same thing.","why":"Quiet means making little sound, and loud means making a lot. They are opposites.","hint":"Picture a library and a loud drum.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-1-005","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"number","q":"An octopus has three hearts. Two hearts help its gills. One heart helps the rest of its body. How many hearts does an octopus have?","why":"The first sentence says it directly. Two hearts work with the gills and one works for the body.","hint":"Reread the first sentence.","tags":["ocean"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-1-006","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Ben dropped his ice cream on the ground. Tears filled his eyes. How did Ben feel?","why":"Tears and a dropped treat are clues. Ben is sad, not proud.","hint":"What do tears usually show?","tags":[],"opts":["proud","sleepy","sad"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-1-007","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is a fact, something that can be proven?","why":"You can check stitches by looking. Best and yummy are feelings, so they are opinions.","hint":"Can you check it by looking?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["A baseball has red stitches.","Baseball is the best game ever.","Hot dogs at games taste yummy."],"a":0},{"id":"rea-1-008","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in order from quietest to loudest.","why":"A whisper is very soft, talking is in the middle, and shouting is very loud.","hint":"Which one would you use in a library?","tags":[],"opts":["whisper","talk","shout"]},{"id":"rea-1-009","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A shark has rows of teeth. When one falls out, a new one grows in. What is this mostly about?","why":"Every sentence talks about teeth, so teeth is the main idea.","hint":"What do all the sentences talk about?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["baby sharks","shark teeth","shark food"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-1-010","topic":"reading","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'unfair' means 'not fair.'","why":"The prefix un- means not. So unfair means not fair.","hint":"What does un- at the start of a word do?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-2-001","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The shark glided through the water without a splash. What does glided mean?","why":"The clue is 'without a splash.' Gliding is smooth and easy, not bumpy or noisy.","hint":"Use the clue 'without a splash.'","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["splashed around loudly","turned and bumped hard","moved smoothly"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-2-002","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put the rocket story in order.","why":"A rocket waits ready, counts down, launches, and then travels around Earth.","hint":"What happens right before blast-off?","tags":["space"],"opts":["The rocket sat on the launch pad.","The countdown began.","The rocket blasted off.","It circled high above Earth."]},{"id":"rea-2-003","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Before the big game, Ava's hands shook. She kept checking her glove. How did she feel?","why":"Shaky hands and checking her glove are clues she was worried, not bored or relaxed.","hint":"What do shaky hands often show?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["nervous","bored","relaxed"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-2-004","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A crab that scurried across the sand moved slowly.","why":"Scurry means to run fast with quick little steps. A slow mover would plod or creep.","hint":"Picture a crab running away from a wave.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-2-005","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"number","q":"How many sentences are in this passage? The whale dove deep. It stayed under for a long time. Then it rose to breathe.","why":"Each sentence ends with a period: dove deep, long time, and breathe.","hint":"Count the periods.","tags":["ocean"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-2-006","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Some dinosaurs ate plants. Others ate meat. What is the main idea?","why":"Both sentences tell what dinosaurs ate. Only one answer covers plants and meat.","hint":"Which answer fits both sentences?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["All dinosaurs ate only plants.","Dinosaurs ate different foods.","Meat tasted better than plants."],"a":1},{"id":"rea-2-007","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in order from slowest to fastest.","why":"Crept is very slow and sprinted is full speed. Walked and jogged fall in between.","hint":"Which one is full speed?","tags":[],"opts":["crept","walked","jogged","sprinted"]},{"id":"rea-2-008","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A shark's skin is covered with tiny scales shaped like teeth. What are the tiny scales shaped like?","why":"They are called denticles, which means 'little teeth.' They make shark skin feel rough.","hint":"Read the first sentence again.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["stars","leaves","teeth"],"a":2,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-2-009","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"'The Moon is the prettiest thing in the sky' is an opinion.","why":"Prettiest is a feeling, and people can disagree. A fact can be checked.","hint":"Can everyone prove it, or can people disagree?","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-2-010","topic":"reading","level":2,"type":"number","q":"At the aquarium, Kai saw hammerhead sharks, nurse sharks, and a whale shark. How many different kinds of shark did he see?","why":"Hammerhead, nurse, and whale shark are three kinds. Count the kinds, not each animal.","hint":"Count the kinds, not the sharks.","tags":["sharks"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-3-001","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The octopus camouflaged itself against the rocks. Hungry sharks swam right past it. What does camouflaged mean?","why":"Camouflaged means hidden by looking like the background. That is why the sharks swam right past.","hint":"Why did the sharks not notice the octopus?","tags":["ocean","sharks"],"opts":["blended in with the rocks","swam off at top speed","squirted ink all over","grew larger to look scary"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-3-002","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The huge dinosaur ____ through the thick mud. Which word shows it moved slowly and heavily?","why":"Trudged means walking with slow, heavy steps. Skipped, pranced, and zipped are light or quick.","hint":"Think about walking in sticky mud.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["skipped","trudged","pranced","zipped"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-3-003","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put the fossil story in order.","why":"Fossils form slowly: burial first, then rock forming over time, and only later can people dig them up.","hint":"Which step needs the most time?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["A dinosaur died near a muddy river.","Mud covered its bones.","Over millions of years, the bones turned to rock.","A scientist dug up the fossil."]},{"id":"rea-3-004","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The coach let the pitcher rest because his arm was sore. What was the cause of the rest?","why":"The word because points to the cause. His sore arm made the coach give him a rest.","hint":"Find the word because.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The other team scored a run","The ball got lost in the grass","His arm was sore","The umpire called a time-out"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-3-005","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Read: Sharks breathe underwater with gills. Dolphins come up to breathe air. True or false: Dolphins breathe with gills.","why":"Dolphins are mammals and breathe air at the surface. Sharks are the ones with gills.","hint":"Reread what the text says dolphins do.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-3-006","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"number","q":"Read: (1) The Sun is a star. (2) The Sun is the prettiest star. (3) The Sun gives Earth light and heat. How many sentences are facts?","why":"Sentences 1 and 3 can be checked. 'Prettiest' is an opinion because people can disagree.","hint":"Which sentence can people disagree about?","tags":["space"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-3-007","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The suffix -less means 'without.' What does fearless mean?","why":"-less means without. Do not mix it up with -ful, which means full of.","hint":"Swap -less for the word without.","tags":[],"opts":["full of fear","fear again","afraid of fear","without fear"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-3-008","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which of these is a real English word?","why":"Kerfuffle means a fuss or commotion, and it is in dictionaries. The other three are made up.","hint":"One of these sounds silly but is in dictionaries.","tags":[],"opts":["kerfuffle","blorptic","zimbrel","quantiffle"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-3-009","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"number","q":"In Triceratops, tri- means 'three' and ceratops means 'horned face.' How many horns did a Triceratops have?","why":"Two long brow horns plus one nose horn make three, matching the tri- in its name.","hint":"Use the meaning of tri-.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-3-010","topic":"reading","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A predator hunts other animals. Prey are the animals that get hunted. True or false: A seal can be prey for a shark.","why":"Some sharks hunt seals, so a seal is prey in that case. Predator and prey are jobs in a food chain.","hint":"Ask yourself who is hunting whom.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-4-001","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"After her perfect science talk, the teacher said Mia hit it out of the park. What does that mean?","why":"A home run that leaves the park is a huge success, so the idiom means doing something extremely well.","hint":"A home run is the best hit in baseball.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["She threw a ball very far","She did an excellent job","She left school early","She broke a window"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-4-002","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Hundreds of small fish suddenly turned together and darted away. A large, dark shape glided past. What most likely made the fish flee?","why":"A large shape gliding past is the clue. Small fish flee together when a predator comes near.","hint":"What does a large dark shape suggest?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["a sudden thunderstorm rolling in","a sudden drop in water temperature","a bigger animal hunting nearby","a swarm of tiny glowing plankton"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-4-003","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"number","q":"Mara said, 'I'm so hungry I could eat a horse!' How many horses will Mara really eat?","why":"This is hyperbole, a big exaggeration. She just means she is very hungry.","hint":"Is she telling the exact truth?","tags":["food"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-4-004","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"In the words 'dehydrate' and 'hydrant,' the root hydr- means water.","why":"Hydr- comes from the Greek word for water. Dehydrate means to lose water, and a hydrant supplies it.","hint":"Think about what a fire hydrant holds.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-4-005","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"On Earth, Zoe's spacesuit felt heavy. On the Moon, the same suit felt light and she bounced when she walked. What best explains this?","why":"The Moon pulls less strongly than Earth, so things weigh less there. Air and warmth do not change weight.","hint":"What force pulls things toward a planet?","tags":["space"],"opts":["The Moon has much thicker air","Her suit shrank during the trip","The Moon is much warmer than Earth","The Moon's gravity is weaker"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-4-006","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put the sea turtle's life story in order.","why":"Eggs come first, then hatching, then the trip to the water, and growing up at sea.","hint":"What must happen before babies can crawl?","tags":["ocean","animals"],"opts":["A mother turtle lays eggs on the beach.","The baby turtles hatch.","They crawl to the sea.","They grow up in the ocean."]},{"id":"rea-4-007","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A baseball glove has a deep pocket to catch the ball and long fingers to reach it. A leather web helps scoop it up. What is the main idea?","why":"Every sentence describes a glove part that helps catch. The other choices are never discussed.","hint":"What do all three sentences have in common?","tags":["baseball","machines"],"opts":["how gloves catch baseballs","how pitchers throw curveballs","why leather is expensive","when baseball first began"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-4-008","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The word astronaut comes from Greek words meaning 'star' and 'sailor.' What does astronaut literally mean?","why":"Astro means star and naut means sailor, so an astronaut is a 'star sailor.'","hint":"Combine the two Greek meanings.","tags":["space"],"opts":["moon walker","star sailor","sky builder","rocket driver"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-4-009","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"number","q":"The prefix bi- means two and tri- means three. How many wheels do a bicycle and a tricycle have in all?","why":"A bicycle has two wheels (bi-) and a tricycle has three (tri-). Two plus three is five.","hint":"Find each prefix, then add.","tags":["machines"],"a":5},{"id":"rea-4-010","topic":"reading","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"'It's raining cats and dogs' means that pets are falling from the sky.","why":"It is an idiom. It means rain is falling very hard, not that animals are falling.","hint":"Idioms do not mean exactly what the words say.","tags":["weather","animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-5-001","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Tele- means 'far.' Phone means 'sound,' and vis- means 'see.' What does the word television literally mean?","why":"Tele- (far) plus vis- (see) means seeing from far away. Sound from far away would be a telephone.","hint":"Match each root to its meaning.","tags":["space"],"opts":["sound from far away","light from a faraway star","sight from far away","a long, flat screen"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-5-002","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"'The Moon was a silver coin in the dark sky' is a simile.","why":"A simile uses like or as. This sentence says the Moon was a coin, with no like or as, so it is a metaphor.","hint":"Look for the words like or as.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-5-003","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in order from the smallest amount of water to the largest.","why":"A drop is one bead. A trickle is a thin flow, a stream is steady, and a torrent is a rushing flood.","hint":"Which word sounds like a flood?","tags":["ocean","weather"],"opts":["drop","trickle","stream","torrent"]},{"id":"rea-5-004","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Scientists found seashell fossils on a high mountain. Long ago, that rock lay on a sea floor. Slow movements of Earth's crust pushed it upward. What lifted the shells?","why":"The passage names the cause: the crust slowly pushed the rock upward. Nothing in the text mentions waves or people.","hint":"Find the sentence that says what pushed the rock.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Giant waves tossing them up there","Ancient people carried them up","Seabirds dropping them from above","Slow movements of the crust"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-5-005","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is an opinion instead of a fact?","why":"Best is a feeling that people can disagree about. The other three can be checked in a rule book.","hint":"Which one has a judgment word like best?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The seventh-inning stretch is the best part.","A regulation baseball weighs about five ounces.","A pitcher throws from a raised mound.","A regular game has nine innings."],"a":0},{"id":"rea-5-006","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"number","q":"The root bio- means 'life.' How many of these words contain it: biology, bicycle, biome, antibiotic, biography?","why":"Biology, biome, antibiotic, and biography all have bio-. Bicycle only has bi-, which means two.","hint":"Look for the exact letters b-i-o.","tags":[],"a":4},{"id":"rea-5-007","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The word dinosaur comes from two Greek words. Deinos means 'terrible' and sauros means 'lizard.' What does dinosaur mean?","why":"Putting the roots together gives 'terrible lizard.' Thunder lizard is the meaning of brontosaurus, a different name.","hint":"Combine the two Greek meanings in order.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["thunder lizard from the sky","terrible lizard","giant swimming reptile","fierce prehistoric bird"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-5-008","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The shortstop was on thin ice with his coach after missing three practices. What does on thin ice mean?","why":"On thin ice means close to getting in trouble. Missing practice puts him in a risky spot.","hint":"Why might a coach be unhappy?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["playing very well","standing in cold air","in a risky spot","ready to be promoted"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-5-009","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Put these sentences in the order a hurricane forms.","why":"Heat from the ocean comes first. Rising air starts the spin, the storm strengthens, and only later does it arrive.","hint":"What gives a hurricane its energy at the start?","tags":["weather","ocean"],"opts":["Warm ocean water heats the air above it.","The warm air rises, and winds begin to spin.","The spinning storm grows stronger.","The hurricane reaches land."]},{"id":"rea-5-010","topic":"reading","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Jada trained all spring for the big game. When the coach read the lineup, her name was missing. Her shoulders sagged and she stared at the floor. How does Jada feel?","why":"Sagging shoulders and staring down are clues of sadness over a letdown, not joy, relief, or loud anger.","hint":"Which clue shows her body language?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["thrilled and proud","angry enough to yell","relieved and calm","disappointed"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-6-001","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The word planet comes from a Greek word meaning what?","why":"Ancient watchers saw planets slowly move against the fixed stars, so the Greeks called them wanderers.","hint":"Think about how planets move across the sky.","tags":["space"],"opts":["wanderer","ball of fire","sky shepherd","night lantern"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-6-002","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A coach praises a pitcher who keeps working hard after losing. Which word gives the most positive feeling? 'He is a ____ player.'","why":"All four words describe someone who will not give up, but only determined sounds like praise. The others sound like complaints.","hint":"Which word would you like to hear about yourself?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["stubborn","determined","pigheaded","bullheaded"],"a":1},{"id":"rea-6-003","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"number","q":"How many of these words begin with a prefix meaning 'not': unlucky, impossible, dishonest, review, incorrect?","why":"Un-, im-, dis-, and in- can all mean not. Re- in review means again, so it does not count.","hint":"Check what each beginning part means.","tags":[],"a":4},{"id":"rea-6-004","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The word quarantine comes from an old Italian word for 'forty,' because ships once waited about forty days before landing.","why":"Quaranta giorni means forty days in Italian. Port cities made ships wait before sailors could come ashore.","hint":"Think of an old number word.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"rea-6-005","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Researchers found three fossil bones. The lead scientist said, 'This is only the tip of the iceberg.' What does she mean?","why":"Most of an iceberg is underwater. The idiom means you see only a small part of something much bigger.","hint":"Most of an iceberg is below the water.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["The bones came from frozen ground","The dig is almost finished for the year","There is much more still hidden","The find is not very important"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-6-006","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Read: (1) Saturn has rings. (2) Saturn's rings are gorgeous. (3) Saturn is a gas giant. (4) Everyone should see Saturn. How many sentences are facts?","why":"Sentences 1 and 3 can be checked by scientists. Gorgeous and should are judgments, so those are opinions.","hint":"Ask which sentences could be tested or measured.","tags":["space"],"a":2},{"id":"rea-6-007","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Paleo- means 'ancient' and -ology means 'study of.' A paleontologist is a scientist who studies what?","why":"Paleontologists study fossils of ancient plants and animals. Ancient buildings are studied by archaeologists.","hint":"Build the meaning from the roots.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["old books","ancient buildings","the oldest stars","ancient life"],"a":3},{"id":"rea-6-008","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in order from least sure to most sure.","why":"Maybe means it might happen. Probably means it likely will, and definitely means there is no doubt.","hint":"Which word leaves no doubt?","tags":[],"opts":["maybe","probably","definitely"]},{"id":"rea-6-009","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Scientists found fossil footprints: many small tracks beside a few large ones, all heading the same way. Which inference is best supported by this clue?","why":"Tracks of different sizes going the same direction suggest a group. The clue does not show hunting or a flood.","hint":"Which answer fits only what the clue shows?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Different sizes may have traveled as a herd","The small dinosaurs were hunting the big ones","A sudden flood forced them all to run","One giant dinosaur made every track"],"a":0},{"id":"rea-6-010","topic":"reading","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Bamboozle is a real English word that means to trick or confuse someone.","why":"Bamboozle is in dictionaries. It has been used in English for centuries to mean fool or puzzle someone.","hint":"It sounds silly, but check the meaning.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wds-1-001","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which spelling is right for the fish with a big fin and sharp teeth?","why":"The sound 'ar' in shark is spelled with the letters a and r, like car and park.","hint":"Think of the word park.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["shark","sherk","shurk"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-1-002","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"One crab is on the rock. Now there are two. Which word tells about two?","why":"Most words add an s to mean more than one. Crab becomes crabs.","hint":"What small letter do we add for more than one?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["crabes","crabs","craby"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-1-003","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"number","q":"How many syllables (claps) are in the word 'octopus'?","why":"Clap it out: oc-to-pus. That is three claps, so three syllables.","hint":"Clap once for each beat in the word.","tags":["ocean"],"a":3},{"id":"wds-1-004","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which word needs a capital letter? 'Today my friend sam hit a home run.'","why":"Names of people start with a capital letter. Friend and home are common words.","hint":"Which word is somebody's name?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["friend","home","sam"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-1-005","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these sea animals in ABC order.","why":"ABC order follows the alphabet by first letter: c, e, s, w.","hint":"Say the alphabet and find each first letter.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["crab","eel","seal","whale"]},{"id":"wds-1-006","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"The words 'sun' and 'son' sound the same but have different meanings.","why":"Words that sound alike but mean different things are called homophones.","hint":"Say both words out loud.","tags":["space"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-1-007","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Which word is a compound word, made from two small words?","why":"Starfish is star plus fish. Elephant and dinosaur are not made from two smaller words.","hint":"Look for two small words inside.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["starfish","elephant","dinosaur"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-1-008","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"number","q":"How many letters are in the word 'baseball'?","why":"Count each letter: b-a-s-e-b-a-l-l. That makes eight.","hint":"Touch each letter as you count.","tags":["baseball"],"a":8},{"id":"wds-1-009","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Yesterday, the whale ____ out of the water. Which spelling is right?","why":"We write the past with -ed, even when it sounds like a t.","hint":"Think about how we spell the past.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["jumpted","jumped","jumpd"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-1-010","topic":"words","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"'Tooths' is the right way to say more than one tooth.","why":"Tooth is an irregular word. We say teeth, not tooths, when there is more than one.","hint":"Say 'one tooth, two ___.'","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-2-001","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which is the right spelling for the smart sea animal that clicks and squeaks?","why":"The sound f in dolphin is spelled ph, like in phone. The ending is -in.","hint":"Think of phone for the f sound.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["dolfin","dolphen","dolphin"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-2-002","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The shark's ____ swished from side to side. Which word fits?","why":"Tail is the part at the end of an animal. A tale is a story.","hint":"Which word is about a body part?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["tail","tale","tayl"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-2-003","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"number","q":"How many words are in this sentence? The big shark swam fast.","why":"Count each word: The, big, shark, swam, fast. That makes five.","hint":"Point to each word as you count.","tags":["sharks"],"a":5},{"id":"wds-2-004","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Yesterday, the pitcher ____ the ball hard. Which word is correct?","why":"Throw is an irregular verb. Its past is threw, not throwed.","hint":"Say it out loud: 'Yesterday I ___.'","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["throwed","threw","thrown"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-2-005","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put these in ABC order.","why":"All start with m, so check the second letter: a, e, e, o. Then the third: r before t.","hint":"They all start with the same letter.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Mars","Mercury","meteor","moon"]},{"id":"wds-2-006","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"What is the short way to write 'do not'?","why":"A contraction squeezes two words into one. The apostrophe takes the place of the missing o.","hint":"Where did the letter o go?","tags":[],"opts":["dont","do'nt","don't"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-2-007","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"You can call more than one fish just 'fish.'","why":"Fish is a special plural. We say 'two fish' when we mean several of the same kind.","hint":"Say 'one fish, two ___.'","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wds-2-008","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Put the sentences in story order.","why":"An egg is laid first, then it waits, then it cracks, and only then can a baby peep out.","hint":"What must happen before an egg can crack?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["A mother dinosaur laid an egg.","The egg sat in a warm nest.","The egg cracked open.","A baby dinosaur peeped out."]},{"id":"wds-2-009","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which word needs a capital letter? 'We sailed near alaska to see whales.'","why":"Names of places start with a capital letter. Sailed and whales are common words.","hint":"Which word names a place?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["alaska","sailed","whales"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-2-010","topic":"words","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The words 'know' and 'no' sound the same but have different meanings.","why":"Know means to understand. No means not any. They are homophones, and know starts with a silent k.","hint":"Say both words out loud.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-3-001","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which is the right spelling for a giant reptile that lived long ago?","why":"Dinosaur comes from Greek roots: dino plus saur. It ends in -aur, not -ore.","hint":"The ending sounds like 'sore' but is spelled -saur.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["dinasaur","dinosaur","dinosore","dynosaur"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-3-002","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"We can ____ the stars tonight. Which word fits?","why":"See means to look with your eyes. A sea is a big body of salt water.","hint":"Which word is about using your eyes?","tags":["space"],"opts":["sea","seem","see","seen"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-3-003","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"number","q":"How many syllables are in the word 'stegosaurus'?","why":"Clap it out: ste-go-sau-rus. That is four parts.","hint":"Clap once for each beat.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":4},{"id":"wds-3-004","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Yesterday, the shark ____ a fish. Which word is correct?","why":"Eat is an irregular verb. Its simple past is ate. Eaten needs a helper like has or had.","hint":"Say 'Yesterday I ___ lunch.'","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["eated","eaten","eat","ate"],"a":3},{"id":"wds-3-005","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these words in ABC order.","why":"All start with sh, so look at the third letter: a, e, i, o.","hint":"Check the third letter.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["shark","shell","ship","shore"]},{"id":"wds-3-006","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which phrase shows that the glove belongs to Mia?","why":"Add an apostrophe and s to a single owner's name to show it owns something.","hint":"Where does the apostrophe go for one owner?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Mia's glove","Mias glove","Mias' glove","Mia glove's"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-3-007","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Add -ing to 'glide.' Which spelling is correct?","why":"When a word ends in silent e, drop the e before adding -ing.","hint":"What happens to the silent e?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["glideing","gliding","glidding","glydeing"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-3-008","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Put these word parts in order to spell a shark with a wide, flat head.","why":"The parts ham, mer, and head spell hammerhead, a shark with eyes at the sides of its wide head.","hint":"Say the word slowly, one part at a time.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["ham","mer","head"]},{"id":"wds-3-009","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'jumped' has two syllables.","why":"Jumped has only one syllable. The -ed ending does not add a beat unless the word ends in t or d.","hint":"Clap it: how many beats?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-3-010","topic":"words","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?","why":"A question needs a question mark at the end. A period or comma does not fit a question.","hint":"What does a question end with?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Do sharks ever stop swimming.","Do sharks ever stop swimming,","Do sharks ever stop swimming?","Do sharks ever stop swimming"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-4-001","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The whale lifted ____ tail out of the water. Which word fits?","why":"Its with no apostrophe shows belonging. It's always means it is or it has.","hint":"Try saying 'it is tail.' Does that work?","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["it's","its'","it","its"],"a":3},{"id":"wds-4-002","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"number","q":"How many vowel letters (a, e, i, o, u) are in the word 'astronaut'?","why":"The vowels are a, o, a, and u. The other letters are consonants.","hint":"Go through the word one letter at a time.","tags":["space"],"a":4},{"id":"wds-4-003","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The shark has ____ across the bay many times. Which word is correct?","why":"After has, have, or had, use swum. Swam is for the simple past, like 'yesterday it swam.'","hint":"Does the sentence have the helper word has?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["swum","swam","swimmed","swimmen"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-4-004","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"The baseball team celebrated ____ best season ever. Which word fits?","why":"Their shows that something belongs to them. There means a place, and they're means they are.","hint":"Whose best season was it?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["there","their","they're","thare"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-4-005","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Put these space words in order from the fewest syllables to the most.","why":"Star has one beat, plan-et two, as-ter-oid three, and con-stel-la-tion four.","hint":"Clap out each word.","tags":["space"],"opts":["star","planet","asteroid","constellation"]},{"id":"wds-4-006","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which is the correct spelling of the word that means 'to spell wrongly'?","why":"The prefix mis- is added to the whole word spell, so there are two s letters in a row and two l's.","hint":"Add mis- to the word spell without dropping a letter.","tags":[],"opts":["mispell","misspel","misspell","misspelle"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-4-007","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"In the contraction 'doesn't,' the apostrophe takes the place of the letter e.","why":"Does not becomes doesn't. The missing letter is the o in not.","hint":"Which word lost a letter, does or not?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-4-008","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"number","q":"How many of these words end with a suffix meaning 'without': fearless, careless, helpful, endless, joyful?","why":"Fearless, careless, and endless end in -less, which means without. Helpful and joyful end in -ful, which means full of.","hint":"Look at the last four letters of each.","tags":[],"a":3},{"id":"wds-4-009","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Three players each have a glove. Which sentence is correct?","why":"For a plural that already ends in s, add just an apostrophe after the s to show owning.","hint":"There is more than one owner.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["The player's gloves are on the bench.","The players gloves are on the bench.","The players's gloves are on the bench.","The players' gloves are on the bench."],"a":3},{"id":"wds-4-010","topic":"words","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence has the correct punctuation?","why":"Put a comma after an interjection like wow. End a strong statement with an exclamation mark.","hint":"Where does a short pause belong?","tags":["space"],"opts":["Wow, that comet is bright!","Wow that comet is bright!","Wow, that comet is bright?","Wow. that comet is bright!"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-5-001","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The Moon's pull has a strong ____ on ocean tides. Which word fits?","why":"Effect is usually the noun, meaning a result. Affect is usually the verb meaning to change something.","hint":"Is the missing word a thing or an action?","tags":["space","ocean"],"opts":["affect","effect","affekt","efect"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-5-002","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"number","q":"How many syllables are in the word 'carnivorous'?","why":"Clap it out: car-niv-o-rous. That is four syllables.","hint":"Clap once for each beat.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":4},{"id":"wds-5-003","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Sharks have many senses, ____ smell. Which spelling is correct?","why":"Especially starts with es, not ex, and ends with -ally, since the base word is especial.","hint":"It begins like the word escape.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["expecially","especialy","especially","especally"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-5-004","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence is in the past tense?","why":"Streaked tells about something that already happened. The other sentences are present or future.","hint":"Which sentence tells about something already over?","tags":["space"],"opts":["The comet streaks across the sky.","The comet will streak across the sky.","The comet is streaking across the sky.","The comet streaked across the sky."],"a":3},{"id":"wds-5-005","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"number","q":"How many of these words are spelled correctly: friend, freind, believe, beleive, receive?","why":"Friend, believe, and receive are right. The rule: i before e, except after c, as in receive.","hint":"Remember i before e, except after c.","tags":[],"a":3},{"id":"wds-5-006","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"'Swimming in the deep sea.' is a complete sentence.","why":"It has no subject telling who is swimming, so it is a fragment. 'Sharks are swimming in the deep sea' is complete.","hint":"A sentence needs someone doing something.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-5-007","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence uses they're, their, and there correctly?","why":"They're means they are, and their shows belonging. There names a place.","hint":"Replace each with 'they are' to test.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["They're hoping their team wins.","Their hoping they're team wins.","There hoping their team wins.","They're hoping there team wins."],"a":0},{"id":"wds-5-008","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"order","q":"These four ocean animals all start with st. Put them in ABC order.","why":"All begin with st, so check the third letter: a, i, o, u.","hint":"The first two letters match, so look at the third.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["starfish","stingray","stonefish","sturgeon"]},{"id":"wds-5-009","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Add -ly to 'hungry' to tell how the shark ate. Which spelling is right?","why":"When a word ends in y after a consonant, change the y to i before adding -ly.","hint":"What happens to the y?","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["hungryly","hungrily","hungrly","hungryily"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-5-010","topic":"words","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"The plural of 'moose' is 'moose.'","why":"Moose does not change. Deer, sheep, and fish also keep the same form for one or many.","hint":"Think about 'one moose, two ___.'","tags":["animals"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wds-6-001","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which is the correct spelling of the word that means 'needed'?","why":"Remember: one collar (c) and two sleeves (ss). Necessary has one c and two s's.","hint":"One collar, two sleeves.","tags":[],"opts":["neccessary","necesary","necessary","neccesary"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-6-002","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Put these word parts in order to spell the scientist who studies stars.","why":"Astronomer comes from Greek words for star and law: as-tron-o-mer.","hint":"Say the word slowly and listen to each part.","tags":["space"],"opts":["as","tron","o","mer"]},{"id":"wds-6-003","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A loud crowd can ____ a pitcher's focus. Which word fits?","why":"Affect is usually the verb, meaning to change. Effect is usually the noun, meaning a result.","hint":"Is the missing word an action?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["effect","affact","effekt","affect"],"a":3},{"id":"wds-6-004","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence uses who's and whose correctly?","why":"Who's means who is. Whose shows belonging. Test: 'Who is the player?' works, and 'who is bat' does not.","hint":"Try replacing each with 'who is.'","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Who's the player whose bat broke?","Whose the player who's bat broke?","Who's the player who's bat broke?","Whose the player whose bat broke?"],"a":0},{"id":"wds-6-005","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"number","q":"How many letters are in the word 'tyrannosaurus'?","why":"Count carefully: t-y-r-a-n-n-o-s-a-u-r-u-s. It has two n's and thirteen letters in all.","hint":"Write the letters down and count them.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":13,"fun":true},{"id":"wds-6-006","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"The word 'conscience' has the whole word 'science' hiding inside it.","why":"Look at the letters: con-SCIENCE. The word science is spelled right there in the second half.","hint":"Read the last seven letters.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"wds-6-007","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which spelling is correct for the word meaning 'without a doubt'?","why":"Definitely has the word finite hiding inside it: de-FINITE-ly. It is never spelled with 'ately.'","hint":"Look for the word finite inside.","tags":[],"opts":["definately","definitely","definitly","defanitely"],"a":1},{"id":"wds-6-008","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"order","q":"These words share their first letters. Put them in ABC order, checking each letter in turn.","why":"Comet comes first (e before m). Comma is a shorter version of command, so it comes first. Then commit (a before i).","hint":"Compare letter by letter. A shorter word comes first.","tags":["space"],"opts":["comet","comma","command","commit"]},{"id":"wds-6-009","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which spelling is right for 'to keep things apart'?","why":"Separate has a rat in the middle: sepARATe. That helps you remember the a.","hint":"Look for a small animal hiding in the middle.","tags":[],"opts":["seperate","separete","separate","seperete"],"a":2},{"id":"wds-6-010","topic":"words","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Which sentence uses commas correctly?","why":"Extra information in the middle of a sentence is set off by a comma before and after it.","hint":"The extra phrase needs a comma on both sides.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["The hammerhead a shark, eats rays.","The hammerhead, a shark eats rays.","The, hammerhead a shark, eats rays.","The hammerhead, a shark, eats rays."],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-1-001","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What comes next? 🦈 🐟 🦈 🐟 🦈 ❓","why":"The pattern is shark, fish, shark, fish. After a shark comes a fish.","hint":"Say the pattern out loud. Which two repeat?","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["🦈","🐟","🐙"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-1-002","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Bat, mitt and snorkel: which one is NOT used to play baseball?","why":"A bat hits the ball and a mitt catches it. A snorkel is for swimming.","hint":"Think about what players use on the field.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Bat","Mitt","Snorkel"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-1-003","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"number","q":"🪐 ⭐ 🪐 ⭐ 🪐 ⭐ 🪐 ⭐  How many ⭐ are in the row?","why":"Count only the stars. They sit between the planets: 1, 2, 3, 4.","hint":"Touch each ⭐ and count. Skip the 🪐.","tags":["space"],"a":4},{"id":"log-en-1-004","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"What comes next? 🔴 🔴 🟦 🔴 🔴 ❓","why":"The pattern is two reds, then one blue. We just had two reds, so blue is next.","hint":"Look for the group that keeps repeating.","tags":[],"opts":["🟦","🔴","🟢"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-1-005","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put these sea animals in order from smallest to biggest.","why":"A shrimp fits in your hand. A turtle is bigger, a great white is bigger still, and a blue whale is giant.","hint":"Start with the one you could hold.","tags":["ocean","sharks"],"opts":["Shrimp","Sea turtle","Great white shark","Blue whale"],"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-1-006","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"Counting by 2s should go 2, 4, 6, 8, 10. Here is a row: 2, 4, 6, 7, 10. Which number is a mistake?","why":"After 6 we add 2 and get 8, not 7. The 7 broke the pattern.","hint":"Add 2 each time and see where it goes wrong.","tags":[],"opts":["4","7","10"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-1-007","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: after 🔺 🔵 🔺 🔵 🔺 🔵 the next shape is 🔺.","why":"The pattern is triangle, circle, triangle, circle. The row ended on a circle, so a triangle is next.","hint":"See which shape the row ended on.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-1-008","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"number","q":"A baseball team has 9 players on the field. 3 of them are in the outfield. How many are NOT in the outfield?","why":"9 take away 3 leaves 6 players. They are the pitcher, catcher and infielders.","hint":"Start at 9 and count back 3.","tags":["baseball"],"a":6},{"id":"log-en-1-009","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"order","q":"Put a baby dinosaur's life in order, first to last.","why":"Dinosaurs started in an egg. They hatched, grew up, and then could lay eggs of their own.","hint":"What comes before hatching?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Egg","Hatchling","Young dinosaur","Grown-up dinosaur"]},{"id":"log-en-1-010","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"🦈 🦈 🦈 🐬 🦈  Which place is the odd one out?","why":"Four animals are sharks and one is a dolphin. The dolphin is in the 4th spot.","hint":"Find the one that is different, then count from the left.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["2nd","3rd","4th"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-1-011","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"number","q":"A rocket countdown goes 5, 4, 3, ❓, 1. What number is missing?","why":"Count backward by 1 each time. After 3 comes 2, and then 1.","hint":"Count backward one step at a time.","tags":["space"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-1-012","topic":"logic","level":1,"type":"choice","q":"A whale is bigger than a crab. A crab is bigger than a shrimp. Which one is the smallest?","why":"The shrimp is smaller than the crab, and the crab is smaller than the whale. So shrimp is smallest.","hint":"Line them up from big to small.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["Whale","Crab","Shrimp"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-2-001","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"number","q":"A rocket counts down by 2s: 30, 28, 26, 24, ❓. What number comes next?","why":"Each number is 2 less than the one before. 24 minus 2 is 22.","hint":"Find how much each number drops.","tags":["space"],"a":22},{"id":"log-en-2-002","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"A dino dig numbers its bones: 3, 8, 13, 18, ❓. What number comes next?","why":"The numbers go up by 5 each time. 18 plus 5 is 23.","hint":"Subtract neighbors to find the jump.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["21","23","24"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-2-003","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Shark A shows 15 teeth. Shark B shows 51 teeth. Which shark shows MORE teeth?","why":"51 has 5 tens and 15 has only 1 ten. The tens digit decides.","hint":"Compare the tens digits first.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["They are the same","Shark A: 15 teeth","Shark B: 51 teeth"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-2-004","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Home runs hit by four players: 18, 9, 31, 24. Put them from fewest to most.","why":"Compare the tens first: 9 is the smallest, then 18, then 24, then 31.","hint":"Find the smallest first, then the next.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["9","18","24","31"]},{"id":"log-en-2-005","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The fins box holds a shark, a tuna and an eagle. Which one is in the wrong box?","why":"Sharks and tuna are fish with fins. An eagle has wings, so it belongs in a different box.","hint":"Ask which animal has no fins.","tags":["sharks","animals"],"opts":["Eagle","Shark","Tuna"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-2-006","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"number","q":"A star pattern grows: group 1 has 1 star, group 2 has 3 stars, group 3 has 5 stars. How many stars are in group 5?","why":"Each group adds 2 stars: 1, 3, 5, 7, 9. Group 5 has 9 stars.","hint":"Find how many stars each group adds.","tags":["space"],"a":9},{"id":"log-en-2-007","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"The even box holds 4, 8 and 12. Which number also belongs in the even box?","why":"Even numbers split into two equal groups. 18 does, but 15 and 21 leave one extra.","hint":"Even numbers end in 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8.","tags":[],"opts":["18","15","21"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-2-008","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"order","q":"Sam throws faster than Lee. Lee throws faster than Ty. Put the pitchers from slowest to fastest.","why":"Ty is slower than Lee and Lee is slower than Sam. So the order is Ty, Lee, Sam.","hint":"Find who is slower than everyone.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Ty","Lee","Sam"]},{"id":"log-en-2-009","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Four of these numbers are even and one is not: 12, 14, 16, 19, 20. Which does NOT belong?","why":"12, 14, 16 and 20 are all even. 19 is odd, so it is the one left out.","hint":"Check the last digit of each number.","tags":[],"opts":["12","20","19"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-2-010","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: if you count by 5s from 5 (5, 10, 15, 20...), you will say the number 18.","why":"Counting by 5s lands on numbers ending in 5 or 0. 18 ends in 8, so you skip it.","hint":"Look at the last digit each time.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-2-011","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"number","q":"The Rangers scored 6 runs. The Waves scored 4 more runs than the Rangers. How many runs did the Waves score?","why":"4 more than 6 is 10. The Waves scored 10 runs.","hint":"Start at 6 and count up 4.","tags":["baseball"],"a":10},{"id":"log-en-2-012","topic":"logic","level":2,"type":"choice","q":"Which shape goes in the empty spot? Row 1: 🔺 🔷 🔺. Row 2: 🔷 🔺 🔷. Row 3: 🔺 🔷 ❓","why":"Every row and column swaps between triangle and diamond. After 🔺 🔷 comes 🔺.","hint":"Look at what is above the empty spot.","tags":[],"opts":["🔷","🔺","🟢"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-3-001","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A moon rover finds this many rocks each day: 2, 5, 8, 11. If the pattern keeps going, how many on day 5?","why":"The pattern adds 3 each day. 11 plus 3 is 14.","hint":"Check how much it grows from day to day.","tags":["space"],"opts":["13","14","15","16"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-3-002","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Jo is taller than Kai. Lee is shorter than Kai. Mo is taller than Jo. Put the four players from shortest to tallest.","why":"Lee is below Kai, Kai is below Jo, and Jo is below Mo. Chain them together.","hint":"Draw a line and place one player at a time.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Lee","Kai","Jo","Mo"]},{"id":"log-en-3-003","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"A fossil hunter's boxes hold 4, 7, 10, 13 bones, and so on. How many bones are in the 7th box?","why":"Each box holds 3 more. After 13 comes 16, 19, then 22 in the 7th box.","hint":"Keep adding the same number, box by box.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":22},{"id":"log-en-3-004","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Each row and column has one 🦈, one 🐙 and one 🐢. Row 1: 🦈 🐙 🐢. Row 2: 🐙 🐢 🦈. Row 3: 🐢 ❓ 🐙. What goes in ❓?","why":"Row 3 already has 🐢 and 🐙, so it needs 🦈. Column 2 agrees.","hint":"Which animal is missing from Row 3?","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["🐬","🐙","🐢","🦈"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-3-005","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"The pattern 🪐 ⭐ ⭐ repeats over and over. Among the first 10 items, how many are ⭐?","why":"Items 1 to 10 are 🪐⭐⭐ 🪐⭐⭐ 🪐⭐⭐ 🪐. That is three groups of 2 stars.","hint":"Write out all 10 items and count.","tags":["space"],"a":6},{"id":"log-en-3-006","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"In a dino parade, Stegosaurus walks behind Triceratops. T. rex walks in front of Triceratops. Who walks last?","why":"The line is T. rex, Triceratops, Stegosaurus. The one behind everybody is last.","hint":"Put the three dinos in a line.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["T. rex","Triceratops","Stegosaurus","Can't tell"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-3-007","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: in the pattern 20, 17, 14, 11, the next number is 8.","why":"Each number is 3 less than the one before. 11 minus 3 is 8.","hint":"Find how much each number drops.","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-3-008","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"order","q":"Work out each sum, then put them from least to greatest.","why":"The sums are 8, 10, 11 and 13. Order the answers, not the first numbers.","hint":"Add first, then compare the answers.","tags":[],"opts":["4 + 4","3 + 7","2 + 9","5 + 8"]},{"id":"log-en-3-009","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"A skip pattern goes 6, 9, 12, 16, 18. Which number is the mistake?","why":"Adding 3 each time gives 6, 9, 12, 15, 18. The 16 should be 15.","hint":"Add the same jump each time to find the break.","tags":[],"opts":["16","9","12","18"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-3-010","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"number","q":"In each row of a number grid, the three numbers add up to 12. Row 1: 5, 4, 3. Row 2: 6, ❓, 2. What number is ❓?","why":"6 plus 2 is 8. To reach 12 you need 4 more.","hint":"First add the two numbers you can see.","tags":[],"a":4},{"id":"log-en-3-011","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"Three of these are sharks. Which one is NOT a shark?","why":"Hammerheads, great whites and whale sharks are fish. An orca is really a dolphin, a mammal that breathes air.","hint":"Think about which are fish and which breathe air.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["Orca","Hammerhead","Great white","Whale shark"],"a":0,"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-3-012","topic":"logic","level":3,"type":"choice","q":"The letters follow a pattern: A B A C A D A ❓. What comes next?","why":"Every other letter is A. The letters in between go B, C, D, so E is next.","hint":"Look at the letters between the A's.","tags":[],"opts":["B","D","A","E"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-4-001","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"Mystery number: it is more than 20, less than 30, a multiple of 7, and odd. What is it?","why":"The multiples of 7 between 20 and 30 are 21 and 28. Only 21 is odd.","hint":"List the multiples of 7 near 20 first.","tags":[],"a":21},{"id":"log-en-4-002","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"A pitcher's jersey number has two digits. The digits add up to 9, and the tens digit is double the ones digit. What is the number?","why":"Try 6 and 3. They add to 9, and 6 is double 3. So the number is 63.","hint":"Try pairs of digits that add to 9.","tags":["baseball"],"a":63},{"id":"log-en-4-003","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Every shark in an aquarium is gray. Mia sees a gray fish and says it must be a shark. What is wrong with her thinking?","why":"The clue says sharks are gray, not that every gray fish is a shark.","hint":"Does the clue work backward too?","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"opts":["Other kinds of fish can be gray too","Sharks are never gray","Fish cannot be gray","Nothing is wrong"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-4-004","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"Leo has 3 boxes with 4 stickers in each. He says 3 + 4 = 7 stickers. How many stickers does he really have?","why":"Each box has 4, and there are 3 boxes: 4 + 4 + 4 = 12. He added the wrong numbers.","hint":"Add the stickers box by box.","tags":[],"a":12},{"id":"log-en-4-005","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Nia, Omar and Pia each love a different thing: sharks, dinosaurs or space. Nia loves neither sharks nor dinosaurs. Omar does not love sharks. Who loves sharks?","why":"Nia must love space. Omar does not love sharks, so he loves dinosaurs. That leaves Pia with sharks.","hint":"Start with the kid who has the most clues.","tags":["sharks","dinosaurs","space"],"opts":["Nia","Omar","Can't tell","Pia"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-4-006","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Four dinosaurs race. Yan finishes right after Zed. Wes finishes before Zed. Xia finishes after Yan. Put them in finishing order, first to last.","why":"Wes comes before Zed, Yan is right after Zed, and Xia is after Yan. That makes Wes, Zed, Yan, Xia.","hint":"Place the one with a 'before' clue first.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["Wes","Zed","Yan","Xia"]},{"id":"log-en-4-007","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"number","q":"A crab walks from A to B, moving only right or down. B is 2 steps right and 2 steps down from A. How many different shortest paths can it take?","why":"List them: RRDD, RDRD, RDDR, DRRD, DRDR, DDRR. That is 6 paths.","hint":"List the paths in an organized way.","tags":["ocean"],"a":6},{"id":"log-en-4-008","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: All sharks are fish, and a hammerhead is a shark. So a hammerhead must be a fish.","why":"A hammerhead is a shark, and every shark is a fish. So the conclusion must be true.","hint":"Follow the chain one link at a time.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["True","False"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-4-009","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Which number fits ALL the clues? It is between 40 and 60. It is a multiple of 5. The tens digit is 5. The ones digit is odd.","why":"Multiples of 5 with a tens digit of 5 are 50 and 55. Only 55 has an odd ones digit.","hint":"Cross out numbers one clue at a time.","tags":[],"opts":["50","45","60","55"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-4-010","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"order","q":"Four fans leave a baseball game after 1/8, 1/4, 1/2 and 3/4 of it is done. Put them from the first to leave to the last.","why":"Eighths are the smallest pieces, then fourths, then halves. 3/4 is the biggest, so that fan leaves last.","hint":"Picture a pizza cut into pieces.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["1/8","1/4","1/2","3/4"]},{"id":"log-en-4-011","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"Today is Wednesday. A rocket launches in 10 days. What day of the week is the launch?","why":"7 days later is Wednesday again, and 3 more days is Saturday.","hint":"Count a full week first, then the extra days.","tags":["space"],"opts":["Friday","Saturday","Sunday","Monday"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-4-012","topic":"logic","level":4,"type":"choice","q":"A coach says: 'We lost our last 3 games, so we will lose the next one.' What is wrong with that thinking?","why":"The next game is a new game. Earlier losses cannot force a result.","hint":"Does the past control what happens next?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Teams cannot lose 3 in a row","He should say they will win","Nothing is wrong","Past games do not decide the next one"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-5-001","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"If it rains, the baseball game is canceled. The game was NOT canceled. What must be true?","why":"If rain always cancels the game, then no cancellation means there was no rain.","hint":"Test it: what would rain have done to the game?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["It rained","We cannot tell","Rain started late","It did not rain"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-5-002","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A toy shark balances 3 toy fish on a scale. One toy fish balances 2 toy crabs. How many toy crabs balance one toy shark?","why":"One shark equals 3 fish. Each fish equals 2 crabs. So 3 groups of 2 make 6 crabs.","hint":"Turn the shark into fish first.","tags":["sharks","ocean"],"a":6},{"id":"log-en-5-003","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"order","q":"Moon rocks on a balance scale: A is heavier than B. C is lighter than B. D is heavier than A. Order them from lightest to heaviest.","why":"C is below B, B is below A, and A is below D. Chain the clues together.","hint":"Build a line one clue at a time.","tags":["space"],"opts":["C","B","A","D"]},{"id":"log-en-5-004","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"What number comes next in this pattern? 2, 3, 6, 7, 14, 15, ❓","why":"The steps take turns: add 1, then double. 15 doubled is 30.","hint":"Two different steps take turns.","tags":[],"opts":["30","16","28","31"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-5-005","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A fossil hunter doubled her bones, then found 5 more. Now she has 21 bones. How many did she start with?","why":"Work backward: 21 minus 5 is 16. Half of 16 is 8.","hint":"Undo the steps in reverse order.","tags":["dinosaurs"],"a":8},{"id":"log-en-5-006","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"If it rains, the game is canceled. The game was canceled. Did it rain?","why":"Rain causes cancellation, but other things could cancel a game too, such as lightning or a broken field.","hint":"Could anything else cancel a game?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Yes, it must have rained","No, it did not rain","Yes, but only a little","We cannot tell for sure"],"a":3,"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-5-007","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"In a class of 20 kids, 12 like sharks and 10 like dinosaurs. Every kid likes at least one. How many like both?","why":"12 + 10 = 22, which is 2 more than 20 kids. Those 2 were counted twice.","hint":"Add the groups, then compare with the class size.","tags":["sharks","dinosaurs"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-5-008","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A toy starfish and 2 marbles balance 5 marbles on a scale. How many marbles balance one toy starfish?","why":"Take 2 marbles from each side. The starfish balances the 3 marbles left.","hint":"Remove the same thing from both sides.","tags":["ocean"],"opts":["2","3","4","5"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-5-009","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: If a shape is a square, it has 4 sides. This shape has 4 sides, so it must be a square.","why":"Rectangles and diamonds have 4 sides too. Square leads to 4 sides, but 4 sides does not lead to square.","hint":"Can you draw a 4-sided shape that is not a square?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-5-010","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"number","q":"A pattern grows: ⚾ 🧢 ⚾⚾ 🧢 ⚾⚾⚾ 🧢 and so on. Counting from 1, in which spot is the 5th 🧢?","why":"Caps sit after groups of 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 balls. That is 15 balls and 5 caps, so spot 20.","hint":"Keep writing the pattern out and count each spot.","tags":["baseball"],"a":20},{"id":"log-en-5-011","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"Sam thinks of a number, adds 6, then halves the result. He gets 7. What was his number?","why":"Undo halving: 7 doubled is 14. Undo adding 6: 14 minus 6 is 8.","hint":"Undo the last step first.","tags":[],"opts":["14","20","8","10"],"a":2},{"id":"log-en-5-012","topic":"logic","level":5,"type":"choice","q":"A space show ended at 4:10 PM after running 2 hours and 25 minutes. When did it start?","why":"Back up 2 hours to 2:10. Then back up 25 minutes to 1:45 PM.","hint":"Go back one hour piece, then the minutes.","tags":["space"],"opts":["1:35 PM","1:45 PM","2:15 PM","1:55 PM"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-6-001","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"A toy bat and a toy ball cost $1.10 together. The bat costs $1 more than the ball. How many cents does the ball cost?","why":"Try 5 cents: the bat is $1.05 and the total is $1.10. A 10-cent ball makes the total $1.20.","hint":"Test your first guess by adding both prices.","tags":["baseball"],"a":5,"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-6-002","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Two coins add up to 30 cents. One of them is not a nickel. Which two coins are they?","why":"The quarter is the one that is not a nickel. The other coin IS a nickel. 25 + 5 = 30.","hint":"Read 'one of them' very carefully.","tags":["money"],"opts":["A dime and a nickel","A quarter and a nickel","Two dimes","A quarter and a dime"],"a":1,"fun":true},{"id":"log-en-6-003","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"Finn and Gill are sharks in a riddle. Each always tells the truth or always lies. Finn says: 'At least one of us is a liar.' Who is who?","why":"If Finn were a liar, his sentence would be true. That is impossible. So Finn is honest, and Gill is the liar.","hint":"Test what happens if Finn is a liar.","tags":["sharks"],"opts":["Both always tell the truth","Both always lie","Finn lies and Gill tells the truth","Finn tells the truth and Gill lies"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-6-004","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"6 baseball players shake hands after a game. Every player shakes hands with every other player exactly once. How many handshakes happen?","why":"Each of 6 players shakes 5 hands, which counts every handshake twice: 30 divided by 2 is 15.","hint":"Count one player's handshakes, then fix the double counting.","tags":["baseball"],"a":15},{"id":"log-en-6-005","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A cube net has four squares in a row: A, B, C, D. Square E is above B and square F is below B. Folded into a cube, which square is opposite B?","why":"A, B, C, D wrap around the cube like a belt. Squares two steps apart are opposite, so D faces B.","hint":"Picture the row of four wrapping around a box.","tags":[],"opts":["A","C","F","D"],"a":3},{"id":"log-en-6-006","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"order","q":"Five players bat in order. Rae bats last. Sol bats right before Rae. Pat bats before Quinn. Tia bats before Pat. Put them in batting order, first to last.","why":"Rae is 5th and Sol is 4th. Tia, Pat, Quinn fill spots 1 to 3, with Tia before Pat before Quinn.","hint":"Place the sure spots first, then fill the gaps.","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["Tia","Pat","Quinn","Sol","Rae"]},{"id":"log-en-6-007","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"A 3 by 3 by 3 cube is painted red on the outside, then cut into 27 small cubes. How many small cubes have exactly 2 red faces?","why":"Those are the edge cubes, one in the middle of each of the 12 edges. Corners show 3 red faces.","hint":"Where do two painted faces meet?","tags":[],"a":12},{"id":"log-en-6-008","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A museum has 17 dinosaur skeletons. All but 9 of them are moved to a new hall. How many skeletons did NOT move?","why":"'All but 9' means 9 stayed behind. The other 8 moved. The 17 is a distraction.","hint":"Which ones did NOT move?","tags":["dinosaurs"],"opts":["9","8","17","26"],"a":0},{"id":"log-en-6-009","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"Algae in a tide pool doubles every day. On day 12 it covers the whole pool. On which day was the pool half covered?","why":"One doubling later it fills the pool. So half-covered was one day earlier, on day 11.","hint":"Doubling goes forward. What does half do backward?","tags":["ocean"],"a":11},{"id":"log-en-6-010","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"A dark drawer holds 10 red baseball socks and 10 blue ones, all mixed up. What is the fewest socks you must grab to be SURE of a matching pair?","why":"Two socks might be one of each color. The third sock must match one of the first two.","hint":"What is the worst luck you could have?","tags":["baseball"],"opts":["2","3","4","11"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-6-011","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"choice","q":"True or false: A fair coin lands heads 5 times in a row. On the next flip, tails is more likely.","why":"Each flip is separate. The coin does not remember, so heads and tails are still equally likely.","hint":"Does a coin remember its past flips?","tags":[],"opts":["True","False"],"a":1},{"id":"log-en-6-012","topic":"logic","level":6,"type":"number","q":"8 space rocks look identical, but one is heavier. What is the fewest weighings on a balance scale that is guaranteed to find the heavy one?","why":"Weigh 3 against 3. Balanced means it's in the other 2; otherwise it's in the heavier 3. One more weighing finishes.","hint":"One weighing has 3 outcomes: left, right, balanced.","tags":["space"],"a":2}];
